AUDIOD(1)                        User Commands                       AUDIOD(1)

NAME
       audiod - the LikeOS audio service: mixes every program's sound onto the
       sound card

SYNOPSIS
       audiod [-f] [-v]...  [-c config] [-d device]
       audiod -t [-c config]
       audiod -V

DESCRIPTION
       audiod  owns  the  sound  card.  Programs do not open /dev/audio0 them-
       selves; they connect to the service  through  the  socket  /var/run/au-
       diod/socket  (with  libaudio, the GStreamer element likeosaudiosink, or
       the tools audioplay(1),  eventsound(1)  and  audioctl(1)),  and  audiod
       mixes  all of them into one stream for the hardware.  That is what lets
       a browser video, a music player and a notification sound  play  at  the
       same time, each at its own volume.

       Each program sends its sound in whatever format it has: 8, 16, 24 or 32
       bit integer, 32 or 64 bit float, 1 to 8 channels, any rate from 4000 to
       384000  Hz.  audiod converts it to the card's format, resamples it with
       a windowed-sinc polyphase filter whose ratio is exact (a  stream  never
       drifts  against the card, however long it plays), up- or down-mixes the
       channels with the usual downmix coefficients (centre and surround chan-
       nels folded in at -3 dB, LFE dropped on cards without one), applies the
       stream's volume and the master volume with short ramps so changes never
       click, and limits the sum softly so that  many  loud  streams  together
       compress instead of distorting.

       The card is driven with 25 ms periods by default, 4 of  them,  which
       keeps  the  service's own latency around 100 ms.  After a  few  sec-
       onds  of  silence the card is stopped, so an idle system has no wakeups
       for sound; it starts again the moment a program plays.

       Headphone and line jacks, HDMI and DisplayPort monitors  are  followed:
       when  the card reports a jack or a monitor coming or going, the outputs
       are read again and subscribed clients (audioctl(1) and  desktop  volume
       controls) are told.  An HDMI or DisplayPort output with its own PCM de-
       vice  on the card is selected by moving the stream to that device; when
       its monitor goes away audiod falls back to the analog outputs  and  re-
       turns  once  it is back.  If the card disappears altogether the service
       carries on with a silent output paced by the clock,  so  programs  keep
       playing at the right speed, and looks for the card again every few sec-
       onds.

       The master volume, mute, the chosen output and card survive restarts in
       /var/lib/audiod/state.

OPTIONS
       -f     Stay  in  the  foreground.   This is how init(8) starts it, from
              /etc/inittab: respawn /usr/sbin/audiod -f .  Without it the ser-
              vice detaches and logs to the console.

       -v     Log more: once for informational messages, twice for  debugging.
              By default only warnings and errors are logged.

       -c config
              Read the configuration from config instead of /etc/audiod.conf.

       -d device
              Use  this  sound  device,  overriding the configuration: auto, a
              card number 0 to 7, or a path /dev/audioN or /dev/dsp[N].

       -t     Check the configuration file, print what it sets, and exit: sta-
              tus 0 when it is valid, 1 when it is not.

       -V     Print the version and exit.

CONFIGURATION
       /etc/audiod.conf holds lines of the form key  =  value,  with  comments
       starting  with  # or ;.  Parsing is strict: an unknown key, a key given
       twice, or a value that is not exactly what the key expects rejects  the
       whole  file  with the line number, and the service keeps what it had --
       the built-in defaults at start, the previous configuration  on  SIGHUP.
       The file must belong to root and must not be writable by others, nor by
       a group other than root.

       device auto  (the  first card that plays, preferring the one remembered
              in the state file), a  card  number,  or  /dev/audioN.   Default
              auto.

       rate   The  card's  sample rate, 8000 to 192000, or auto: 48000 Hz when
              the card can, 44100 Hz otherwise.  Default auto.

       format auto, s16, s24, s24_3, s20, s32, float or u8.  Auto takes 16 bit
              when the card has it.

       channels
              1 to 8, or auto (stereo).

       period_ms
              Length of one period, 1 to 100 ms.  Default 25.  Keep it above
              about 16 ms: an emulated card in a virtual machine may  advance
              only one period per host timer tick (often 15.6 ms).

       periods
              Periods in the card's ring, 2 to 32.   Default  4.   The  card's
              buffer, and so the latency it adds, is period_ms times periods.

       default_volume
              Master  volume, 0 to 100, when there is no saved state.  Default
              75.

       resample_quality
              best (64-tap filter, about 100 dB of stop-band rejection),  good
              (32  taps,  about 80 dB; the default) or fast (linear interpola-
              tion, for very slow machines).

       event_sounds
              yes or no: whether event sounds  (start-up,  notifications)  are
              played at all.

       event_rate
              How many event sounds one user may start in 10 seconds.  Default
              10.

       max_streams_per_user
              Streams one user may have open, 1 to 64.  Default 16.

       max_clients
              Connections at once, 1 to 256.  Default 64.  One user may use at
              most three quarters of them.

       max_buffer_kb_per_user
              Stream buffer memory one user may hold, in KiB.  Default 16384.

       default_buffer_ms
              Buffer  of a stream that does not ask for a size, 10 to 2000 ms.
              Default 40.

       suspend_ms
              Stop the card after this much silence; 0 keeps it running.   De-
              fault 5000.

       hw_volume
              auto,  yes or no: whether the master volume moves the card's am-
              plifier (when it has one) or is applied to the samples.  Default
              auto.

       log_level
              error, warning, info or debug.  Default warning.

SECURITY
       audiod starts as root only to create /var/run/audiod (mode 0755,  owner
       audiod:audio)  and /var/lib/audiod (0700), and to open the card and the
       socket.  Then it reduces its groups to audio, sets its group  and  user
       ids  to audio and audiod for good, checks that root cannot be regained,
       changes to / and sets its umask to 077.  It refuses to continue if  any
       of that fails.

       The  socket  is mode 0666: every local user may play sound.  The kernel
       names the user on the other end of every connection (SO_PEERCRED),  and
       that is what decides what a client may do:

       o  anyone  may  play, and may change the master volume, mute and output
          -- it is the person at the machine who turns the sound down;

       o  the volume of another program's  stream  can  be  changed,  and  the
          stream stopped, only by the user who owns it, or by root;

       o  only root may switch to another card;

       o  everyone  sees  the  list of streams, but the titles of other users'
          streams are left out.

       Every message is checked against the protocol before its body is  read;
       a  malformed message, an unknown request, or sound data beyond what the
       service asked for closes that connection.  Connections, streams, buffer
       memory and event sounds are limited per user.  Event sounds are  played
       by    name    only    (letters    a-z,    digits,   _   and   -)   from
       /usr/share/sounds/likeos; no path a client sends is ever opened.

SIGNALS
       SIGTERM
              Fade out over 60 ms, let the card play that, close it, save  the
              state and exit.

       SIGHUP Read  the  configuration again.  When the card settings changed,
              the card is reopened with them; playing streams continue.

       SIGUSR1
              Log the state of the card and of every stream, at warning  level
              so it appears without -v.

FILES
       /etc/audiod.conf
              The configuration.

       /var/run/audiod/socket
              Where clients connect.

       /var/lib/audiod/state
              Master volume, mute, output and card, written atomically.

       /usr/share/sounds/likeos/*.wav
              The event sounds.

       /dev/audio0 /dev/mixer
              The first card; further cards are /dev/audioN and /dev/mixerN.

EXIT STATUS
       0      Stopped  by  SIGTERM  or  SIGINT,  or -t found the configuration
              valid.

       1      Could not start: another audiod is running, the socket directory
              or the socket could not be created, or privileges could  not  be
              dropped.  With -t: the configuration is not valid.

       2      A bad argument.

SEE ALSO
       audioctl(1),  audioplay(1),  audiotest(1), audioinfo(1), eventsound(1),
